@meta
id: 11-12
title: cgn — what Ctrl+D was really for
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: cgn, dot-repeat
par: 37
minutes: 8
vscode: put the cursor on custId and hit Ctrl+D twelve times
open: refund.ts

@brief
`custId` was always meant to be `customerId`. It appears twelve times in
this file — in a type, in arguments, as a shorthand property, in log
calls. Rename every one of them.

This is the job you would have used multiple cursors for. Cursor on the
word, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+D until the whole file is bristling with
carets, then type once and hope you did not pick up a match you did not
want.

Vim's answer is smaller and, once you have it, better. One key puts the
word you are standing on into the search register. One command changes the
next match of that search. And then the key you have spent this whole
chapter on repeats it — find and change, find and change — for as long as
you keep pressing it.

Nothing about it depends on the matches being visible on screen, or on
there being twelve of them rather than two hundred.

@keys
| `*` | search for the word under the cursor, whole word only |
| `gn` | select the next match of the last search |
| `cgn` | change the next match — a normal, ordinary, repeatable change |
| `.` | find the next match and change it too |
| `u` | undo one of them if you go too far |

@hints
Get the cursor onto any `custId` and let vim work out the pattern. You
should not be typing the old name into a search.
--
`*` searches for the word under the cursor. `cgn` then changes the next
match of that search, which makes the rename an ordinary change — and
ordinary changes repeat.
--
`jw` to reach the first `custId`, `*`, then `cgncustomerId` and `Esc`.
From there it is eleven presses of `.` and the file is done.

@solution
    jw  *  cgn customerId <Esc>   then  .  eleven times

Thirty-two keystrokes, ten of which are the new name, and the only thought
you had was "rename this".

Take the three parts separately, because each is useful on its own.

**`*`** searches for the word under the cursor. It wraps it in word
boundaries first, so `custId` will not match inside `custIdList` or
`legacyCustIdMap`. That is usually what you want for a rename and it is
worth knowing it is happening, because it is the difference between this
and a blind find-and-replace. `*` also moves you to the next match, so the
one you were standing on is the last one you will change — the wrap comes
back round to it at the end.

**`gn`** selects the next match of the last search — or the match you are
sitting on, if you are sitting on one. It is a motion-like thing that an
operator can take, so `dgn` deletes the next match, `ygn` yanks it, `gUgn`
upper-cases it.

**`cgn`** is that combination: change the next match. It is one command,
so it is one change, so `.` holds all of it — the jump to the next match,
the delete, and the ten characters you typed. Every `.` from then on finds
the next match on its own. You are not pressing `n` between them; the `gn`
inside the change does the searching.

And the reason this beats multiple cursors is not the keystroke count. It
is that nothing is committed until you commit it. With twelve carets on
screen every match is already selected, and if one of them is wrong you
find out after you type. With `cgn` and `.` you change them one at a time,
each one visible, each one undoable on its own, and if a match should be
left alone you press `n` to step over it instead of `.`. That is the next
lesson but one.

@start refund.ts
export type RefundAllArgs = {
  custId: string;
  reason: string;
};

export async function refundAll(args: RefundAllArgs) {
  const customer = await customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id: args.custId } });

  if (!customer) {
    throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
  }

  const orders = await orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId: args.custId });

  for (const order of orders) {
    await refundOne(order.id, args.custId);
  }

  logger.info('refund batch done', { custId: args.custId, count: orders.length });

  return { custId: args.custId, refunded: orders.length };
}

export async function refundOne(orderId: string, custId: string) {
  const refund = await paymentService.refund(orderId, custId);

  logger.info('refund issued', { orderId, custId });

  if (!refund.ok) {
    throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
  }

  return auditRepository.create({ orderId, custId, at: new Date() });
}

@expected refund.ts
export type RefundAllArgs = {
  customerId: string;
  reason: string;
};

export async function refundAll(args: RefundAllArgs) {
  const customer = await customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id: args.customerId } });

  if (!customer) {
    throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
  }

  const orders = await orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId: args.customerId });

  for (const order of orders) {
    await refundOne(order.id, args.customerId);
  }

  logger.info('refund batch done', { customerId: args.customerId, count: orders.length });

  return { customerId: args.customerId, refunded: orders.length };
}

export async function refundOne(orderId: string, customerId: string) {
  const refund = await paymentService.refund(orderId, customerId);

  logger.info('refund issued', { orderId, customerId });

  if (!refund.ok) {
    throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
  }

  return auditRepository.create({ orderId, customerId, at: new Date() });
}
